    "summary": "Mellow first set, strong Blow Away, Brent shines throughout",
    "blurb": "The concert opens with a relaxed set featuring a crisp El Paso and a brief lyric slip on Bertha. The second set kicks up the energy with a powerful Blow Away, tight Drums>Space transition, and standout vocal work on Tom Thumb's Blues. Brent Mydland’s keyboards dominate, especially on Black Muddy River and Stella Blue.",
    "review": "April 3, 1989 at Pittsburgh’s Civic Arena delivers a mellow first set that eases the crowd in with solid song choices – a clean‑cut El Paso, the ever‑reliable Greatest Story Ever Told (aside from a tiny lyric slip on Bertha) and a nicely paced Jack‑A‑Roe. Jerry’s tone is described as “real cool,” growing more confident as the night progresses, while Bob Weir shines vocally, earning the tongue‑in‑cheek moniker “All Wier.”\n\nThe second set turns up the heat. Blow Away erupts with Brent Mydland’s keyboards front‑and‑center, feeding into a fluid Estimated Prophet → Crazy Fingers → Uncle John’s Band sequence that keeps the band locked in. A spirited Drums>Space segment revives the momentum before the band rolls through Stella Blue and Sugar Magnolia, ending the night on an uplifting encore of Johnny B. Goode and Black Muddy River. Across multiple recordings the show is praised for its strong song selection, Brent’s dominant keys, and Jerry’s engaging leads, despite a few uneven moments noted in some sources.",
